Chapter: GopHid

 USB HID boot-keyboard reports, decoded into Set-1 scancodes (H2b).

 On a machine with no PS/2 controller -- which is most machines made in
 the last decade -- the firmware's keyboard emulation dies at
 ExitBootServices and the boot payload goes deaf. The keys are still
 there, arriving as 8-byte reports on an interrupt endpoint of a USB
 keyboard. This chapter turns those reports into the same Set-1
 scancodes the PS/2 ISR produces, so every consumer above it --
 kb-process-scancode, the wizard's text field, the boot menu -- is
 unchanged and cannot tell the difference.

 The whole chapter is pure. It reads and writes two 8-byte buffers and
 calls nothing effectful, so the decode is battery-tested against report
 sequences taken from the HID specification, with no controller and no
 keyboard. codex/test/hid-decode does exactly that.

 Boot report layout (HID 1.11, Appendix B.1): byte 0 is a modifier
 bitmap, byte 1 is reserved, and bytes 2 through 7 carry up to six
 usage IDs of keys currently held. It is a set, not a queue: nothing in
 the report says which key changed. Usage 0x01 in any slot is
 ErrorRollOver -- more keys are down than the keyboard can report, and
 the whole report is meaningless.

 We say:

Section: One Event At A Time

 A single report transition can imply several events at once: pressing
 Shift and H together yields a Shift make and an H make. The consumers
 above take one scancode at a time from a one-byte mailbox, so this
 chapter hands back one event per call and advances prev one step
 toward cur. No queue, no allocation: prev converges on cur after as
 many calls as there were changes, and a caller that stops early simply
 sees the rest of the events on its next visit.

 The order matters. Modifier presses come before key presses, so Shift
 is down before H arrives; key releases come before modifier releases,
 so H lifts before Shift does. A consumer tracking shift state with
 kb-process-scancode therefore sees exactly the sequence a PS/2
 keyboard would have sent.

Section: Usage Tables

 An independent transcription of the HID Usage Tables, Keyboard/Keypad
 page (0x07), into IBM PC/AT Set-1 make codes. A break code is the make
 code with bit 7 set. Zero means a usage this table does not name; the
 decoder consumes such a key (so prev still converges) and reports no
 scancode for it.

  hid-letter-sc : Integer -> Integer
  hid-letter-sc (u) =
    if u == 4 then 30 else if u == 5 then 48 else if u == 6 then 46
    else if u == 7 then 32 else if u == 8 then 18 else if u == 9 then 33
    else if u == 10 then 34 else if u == 11 then 35 else if u == 12 then 23
    else if u == 13 then 36 else if u == 14 then 37 else if u == 15 then 38
    else if u == 16 then 50 else if u == 17 then 49 else if u == 18 then 24
    else if u == 19 then 25 else if u == 20 then 16 else if u == 21 then 19
    else if u == 22 then 31 else if u == 23 then 20 else if u == 24 then 22
    else if u == 25 then 47 else if u == 26 then 17 else if u == 27 then 45
    else if u == 28 then 21 else if u == 29 then 44
    else 0

  hid-digit-sc : Integer -> Integer
  hid-digit-sc (u) =
    if u == 30 then 2 else if u == 31 then 3 else if u == 32 then 4
    else if u == 33 then 5 else if u == 34 then 6 else if u == 35 then 7
    else if u == 36 then 8 else if u == 37 then 9 else if u == 38 then 10
    else if u == 39 then 11
    else 0

  hid-punct-sc : Integer -> Integer
  hid-punct-sc (u) =
    if u == 40 then 28 else if u == 41 then 1 else if u == 42 then 14
    else if u == 43 then 15 else if u == 44 then 57 else if u == 45 then 12
    else if u == 46 then 13 else if u == 47 then 26 else if u == 48 then 27
    else if u == 49 then 43 else if u == 51 then 39 else if u == 52 then 40
    else if u == 53 then 41 else if u == 54 then 51 else if u == 55 then 52
    else if u == 56 then 53 else if u == 57 then 58
    else 0

 F1 through F10 are contiguous at 59; F11 and F12 are not.

  hid-fn-sc : Integer -> Integer
  hid-fn-sc (u) =
    if u >= 58 & u <= 67 then 59 + (u - 58)
    else if u == 68 then 87 else if u == 69 then 88
    else 0

 The navigation keys a real PS/2 keyboard sends with an 0xE0 prefix.
 Our consumers drop the prefix byte and act on the second byte, so the
 bare make code is exactly what they expect to see.

  hid-nav-sc : Integer -> Integer
  hid-nav-sc (u) =
    if u == 79 then 77 else if u == 80 then 75 else if u == 81 then 80
    else if u == 82 then 72 else if u == 73 then 71 else if u == 74 then 73
    else if u == 75 then 83 else if u == 77 then 79 else if u == 78 then 81
    else 0

  hid-usage-sc : Integer -> Integer
  hid-usage-sc (u) =
    let a = hid-letter-sc u
    in if a /= 0 then a
    else let b = hid-digit-sc u
    in if b /= 0 then b
    else let c = hid-punct-sc u
    in if c /= 0 then c
    else let d = hid-fn-sc u
    in if d /= 0 then d
    else hid-nav-sc u

 Modifier bits, in the order the report packs them: LCtrl, LShift,
 LAlt, LGui, RCtrl, RShift, RAlt, RGui. Only the four that Set-1 names
 without an 0xE2 prefix are mapped. The Gui keys and the right-hand
 Ctrl and Alt are extended keys; reporting nothing for them is honest,
 where reporting their left-hand twin would be a lie.

  hid-mod-sc : Integer -> Integer
  hid-mod-sc (b) =
    if b == 0 then 29 else if b == 1 then 42
    else if b == 2 then 56 else if b == 5 then 54
    else 0

Section: Report Inspection

  hid-bit : Integer -> Integer
  hid-bit (b) = bit-shl 1 b

  hid-mod-set : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  hid-mod-set (buf) (b) = bit-and (peek-byte buf 0) (hid-bit b) /= 0

 A usage of 0 is an empty slot and 1 is ErrorRollOver; both are below
 the first real key (4), so "greater than 3" is the liveness test.

  hid-slot-live : Integer -> Boolean
  hid-slot-live (u) = u > 3

  hid-holds : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  hid-holds (buf) (u) (i) =
    if i > 7 then False
    else if peek-byte buf i == u then True
    else hid-holds buf u (i + 1)

  hid-has-rollover : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  hid-has-rollover (cur) (i) =
    if i > 7 then False
    else if peek-byte cur i == 1 then True
    else hid-has-rollover cur (i + 1)

Section: Finding The Change

 Each of these returns the first difference of its kind, or -1.

  hid-find-mod-press : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  hid-find-mod-press (prev) (cur) (b) =
    if b > 7 then (-1)
    else if hid-mod-set cur b & (hid-mod-set prev b == False) then b
    else hid-find-mod-press prev cur (b + 1)

  hid-find-mod-release : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  hid-find-mod-release (prev) (cur) (b) =
    if b > 7 then (-1)
    else if hid-mod-set prev b & (hid-mod-set cur b == False) then b
    else hid-find-mod-release prev cur (b + 1)

  hid-find-key-press : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  hid-find-key-press (prev) (cur) (i) =
    if i > 7 then (-1)
    else let u = peek-byte cur i
    in if hid-slot-live u & (hid-holds prev u 2 == False) then u
    else hid-find-key-press prev cur (i + 1)

  hid-find-key-release : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  hid-find-key-release (prev) (cur) (i) =
    if i > 7 then (-1)
    else let u = peek-byte prev i
    in if hid-slot-live u & (hid-holds cur u 2 == False) then i
    else hid-find-key-release prev cur (i + 1)

  hid-free-slot : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  hid-free-slot (prev) (i) =
    if i > 7 then (-1)
    else if hid-slot-live (peek-byte prev i) == False then i
    else hid-free-slot prev (i + 1)

Section: Applying The Change

 Each applier mutates prev and returns the scancode it implies, or -1
 for a change that was applied but names no Set-1 code. Returning -1
 rather than 0 keeps "nothing left to do" distinct from "nothing to
 say about this key".

  hid-unmapped : Integer = -1

  hid-do-mod-press : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  hid-do-mod-press (prev) (b) =
    let p = poke-byte prev 0 (bit-or (peek-byte prev 0) (hid-bit b))
    in let sc = hid-mod-sc b
    in if sc == 0 then hid-unmapped else sc

  hid-do-mod-release : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  hid-do-mod-release (prev) (b) =
    let p = poke-byte prev 0 (bit-and (peek-byte prev 0) (255 - hid-bit b))
    in let sc = hid-mod-sc b
    in if sc == 0 then hid-unmapped else bit-or sc 128

 A press with no free slot cannot be recorded, so it must not be
 reported either: prev would never converge and the key would repeat
 forever. Six down is the report's own limit, so this is unreachable
 from a conforming device.

  hid-do-key-press : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  hid-do-key-press (prev) (u) =
    let s = hid-free-slot prev 2
    in if s < 0 then 0
    else let p = poke-byte prev s u
    in let sc = hid-usage-sc u
    in if sc == 0 then hid-unmapped else sc

  hid-do-key-release : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  hid-do-key-release (prev) (i) =
    let u = peek-byte prev i
    in let p = poke-byte prev i 0
    in let sc = hid-usage-sc u
    in if sc == 0 then hid-unmapped else bit-or sc 128

Section: The Step

  hid-apply-one : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  hid-apply-one (prev) (cur) =
    let mp = hid-find-mod-press prev cur 0
    in if mp >= 0 then hid-do-mod-press prev mp
    else let kp = hid-find-key-press prev cur 2
    in if kp >= 0 then hid-do-key-press prev kp
    else let kr = hid-find-key-release prev cur 2
    in if kr >= 0 then hid-do-key-release prev kr
    else let mr = hid-find-mod-release prev cur 0
    in if mr >= 0 then hid-do-mod-release prev mr
    else 0

 An unmapped change is consumed and the search continues, so a key this
 table does not name costs one iteration rather than stalling the
 stream. The fuel bounds that search at more changes than a report can
 possibly carry (eight modifiers plus six keys, pressed and released).

  hid-step-fuel : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  hid-step-fuel (prev) (cur) (fuel) =
    if fuel <= 0 then 0
    else let r = hid-apply-one prev cur
    in if r == hid-unmapped then hid-step-fuel prev cur (fuel - 1)
    else r

 The one entry point. Returns the next Set-1 scancode implied by the
 difference between the last report and this one, or 0 when there is
 nothing left. An ErrorRollOver report is discarded whole, leaving prev
 untouched, so the keys still held are not spuriously released.

  hid-step : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  hid-step (prev) (cur) =
    if hid-has-rollover cur 2 then 0
    else hid-step-fuel prev cur 32
